[0004]    Conventional qMRI methods use analytical solutions of spinevolutions to estimate tissue properties (e.g., T1 and T2 relaxation parameters  SVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.090.107 MRI). These techniques typically implement a series of inversion-recovery or saturation-recovery measurements to estimate the longitudinal (spin-lattice) relaxation time, T1, and spin-echo measurements to estimate the transverseSVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.1570.753
relaxation time, T2. Although such measurements can provideexcellent results and are often used as ground-truth, they require long acquisition times (TA) that are impractical for use in routine clinical examinations. Therefore, alternative methods have been introduced to determine T1 and T2 more rapidly. However, high isotropic spatial resolution and large volume coverage are still limited by long TA and/or signal-to-noise-ratio. Additionally, most of these methods are tailored to estimate a single MR parameter at a time and, in turn, their results are often biased by physical mechanisms that are not accounted for in the model.

[0007]    the first method is the "magnetization-prepared 2 rapid gradient echoes" (MP2RAGE) [4], which uses an inversion pulse to prepare the magnetization in each repetition time (TB), followed by the acquisition of two three-dimensional identical FLASH blocks at different inversion times (TI) (seeFig. 1A). Two images can be reconstructed from one MP2RAGE scan SVG
